  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    from collections import Counter\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    # For each row, find the most common value (the \"background\" color)\n    # Then identify horizontal bands of rows sharing the same background\n    row_modes = []\n    for r in range(rows):\n        c = Counter(grid[r])\n        row_modes.append(c.most_common(1)[0][0])\n    \n    # For column-based bands (Ex1,Ex2) vs row-based bands (Ex3), need to detect orientation\n    # Check if bands are horizontal (rows grouped) or vertical (columns grouped)\n    \n    # Try both and see which gives cleaner bands\n    col_modes = []\n    for c in range(cols):\n        col_vals = [grid[r][c] for r in range(rows)]\n        cnt = Counter(col_vals)\n        col_modes.append(cnt.most_common(1)[0][0])\n    \n    # Fill each cell with the mode of its row or column depending on band structure\n    # Actually: each cell belongs to a region defined by row-band and col-band, fill with the dominant value\n    \n    # Detect row bands: consecutive rows with same mode\n    # Detect col bands: consecutive cols with same mode\n    \n    # For each cell, determine its value from the intersection\n    # Simplest: for each cell, the correct value is determined by which \"band\" it belongs to\n    \n    # Check if rows form bands or columns form bands\n    # In Ex3, rows form bands. In Ex1/Ex2, columns form bands.\n    # Actually in Ex1, both row and column bands exist - it's a grid of rectangular regions.\n    \n    # The output fills each cell with the mode value of that cell's position.\n    # Each cell's output = the dominant color of whichever rectangular region it belongs to.\n    \n    # Simplest approach: for each cell, output = mode of its row AND column intersection\n    # But really, just fill each cell with the value that is most common considering its row-mode and col-mode.\n    \n    # Looking at examples: output[r][c] = the background color of the region containing (r,c).\n    # The region is defined by row-bands and col-bands.\n    \n    # Group rows into bands of same row_mode, group cols into bands of same col_mode.\n    # For each (row_band, col_band) region, find the most common value -> fill.\n    \n    # But actually, looking more carefully, each cell just gets replaced by the mode of its row? No...\n    # In Ex1, row 0 has values from multiple column bands. The output keeps column structure.\n    \n    # Each cell gets the value that is the mode of the rectangular region it belongs to.\n    # Region = intersection of row-band and col-band.\n    \n    def get_bands(modes):\n        bands = []\n        i = 0\n        while i < len(modes):\n            j = i\n            while j < len(modes) and modes[j] == modes[i]:\n                j += 1\n            bands.append((i, j, modes[i]))\n            i = j\n        return bands\n    \n    row_bands = get_bands(row_modes)\n    col_bands = get_bands(col_modes)\n    \n    # For each region, find the most common value\n    region_val = {}\n    for rb in row_bands:\n        for cb in col_bands:\n            vals = []\n            for r in range(rb[0], rb[1]):\n                for c in range(cb[0], cb[1]):\n                    vals.append(grid[r][c])\n            mode_val = Counter(vals).most_common(1)[0][0]\n            region_val[(rb[0], cb[0])] = mode_val\n    \n    result = [row[:] for row in grid]\n    for rb in row_bands:\n        for cb in col_bands:\n            v = region_val[(rb[0], cb[0])]\n            for r in range(rb[0], rb[1]):\n                for c in range(cb[0], cb[1]):\n                    result[r][c] = v\n    return result",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    from collections import Counter\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    # For each row, find the most common value (the \"background\" color)\n    # Then identify horizontal bands of rows sharing the same background\n    row_modes = []\n    for r in range(rows):\n        c = Counter(grid[r])\n        row_modes.append(c.most_common(1)[0][0])\n    \n    # For column-based bands (Ex1,Ex2) vs row-based bands (Ex3), need to detect orientation\n    # Check if bands are horizontal (rows grouped) or vertical (columns grouped)\n    \n    # Try both and see which gives cleaner bands\n    col_modes = []\n    for c in range(cols):\n        col_vals = [grid[r][c] for r in range(rows)]\n        cnt = Counter(col_vals)\n        col_modes.append(cnt.most_common(1)[0][0])\n    \n    # Fill each cell with the mode of its row or column depending on band structure\n    # Actually: each cell belongs to a region defined by row-band and col-band, fill with the dominant value\n    \n    # Detect row bands: consecutive rows with same mode\n    # Detect col bands: consecutive cols with same mode\n    \n    # For each cell, determine its value from the intersection\n    # Simplest: for each cell, the correct value is determined by which \"band\" it belongs to\n    \n    # Check if rows form bands or columns form bands\n    # In Ex3, rows form bands. In Ex1/Ex2, columns form bands.\n    # Actually in Ex1, both row and column bands exist - it's a grid of rectangular regions.\n    \n    # The output fills each cell with the mode value of that cell's position.\n    # Each cell's output = the dominant color of whichever rectangular region it belongs to.\n    \n    # Simplest approach: for each cell, output = mode of its row AND column intersection\n    # But really, just fill each cell with the value that is most common considering its row-mode and col-mode.\n    \n    # Looking at examples: output[r][c] = the background color of the region containing (r,c).\n    # The region is defined by row-bands and col-bands.\n    \n    # Group rows into bands of same row_mode, group cols into bands of same col_mode.\n    # For each (row_band, col_band) region, find the most common value -> fill.\n    \n    # But actually, looking more carefully, each cell just gets replaced by the mode of its row? No...\n    # In Ex1, row 0 has values from multiple column bands. The output keeps column structure.\n    \n    # Each cell gets the value that is the mode of the rectangular region it belongs to.\n    # Region = intersection of row-band and col-band.\n    \n    def get_bands(modes):\n        bands = []\n        i = 0\n        while i < len(modes):\n            j = i\n            while j < len(modes) and modes[j] == modes[i]:\n                j += 1\n            bands.append((i, j, modes[i]))\n            i = j\n        return bands\n    \n    row_bands = get_bands(row_modes)\n    col_bands = get_bands(col_modes)\n    \n    # For each region, find the most common value\n    region_val = {}\n    for rb in row_bands:\n        for cb in col_bands:\n            vals = []\n            for r in range(rb[0], rb[1]):\n                for c in range(cb[0], cb[1]):\n                    vals.append(grid[r][c])\n            mode_val = Counter(vals).most_common(1)[0][0]\n            region_val[(rb[0], cb[0])] = mode_val\n    \n    result = [row[:] for row in grid]\n    for rb in row_bands:\n        for cb in col_bands:\n            v = region_val[(rb[0], cb[0])]\n            for r in range(rb[0], rb[1]):\n                for c in range(cb[0], cb[1]):\n                    result[r][c] = v\n    return result",
